WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:22.140
BLACKOUTS:
When I visited India when I was 8&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:30.120
years old, I was surprised to see that the power&nbsp;
was shut off for a few hours every single day. At&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:35.280
the time, my main concern was: how do I watch&nbsp;
TV or how do I use the internet when the power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:41.160
was off. But, looking back, these were the&nbsp;
least of people’s concerns. No power meant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:46.320
that refrigerators couldn’t be run and that small&nbsp;
businesses and hospitals had to pay for generators&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:52.140
on a daily basis. For many people living there,&nbsp;
this was just a harsh reality of daily life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:58.440
Naturally, you would assume that this is only an&nbsp;
issue in developing countries, but one developed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:04.740
country that’s very much facing this same issue&nbsp;
is none other than Japan. Japan has not only had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:08.220
to implement electricity saving measures like&nbsp;
turning down the heat and dimming the lights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:15.180
but they’ve been experiencing full on blackouts.&nbsp;
The country with internet speeds of 319 terabits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:22.320
per second and bullet trains capable of reaching&nbsp;
603 km/h is struggling to keep the lights on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:28.920
At first glance, you might be driven to think that&nbsp;
this is due to Japan’s economic situation. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:34.200
all, it’s no secret that Japan has been facing&nbsp;
an economic crisis for decades. They have a debt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:40.680
to GDP ratio of 257%, they’re stock market&nbsp;
hasn’t made a new all time high in 33 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:45.960
and the Japanese Yen is at 35 year lows&nbsp;
against the US dollar. All of these would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:50.940
be great reasons for Japan to cut back on power&nbsp;
consumption and try to save some money. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:57.780
their economic crisis is not the main reason that&nbsp;
they’re also facing an energy crisis. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:03.960
Japan would likely be facing an energy crisis even&nbsp;
if they’re economy was booming. The problem would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:08.640
actually probably be worse because people&nbsp;
would likely consume even more power if the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:15.060
economy was booming. But then, if the problem&nbsp;
isn’t being able to pay for it, what is? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:20.700
for Japan, the problem is actually getting&nbsp;
their hands on electricity. For decades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:28.740
80 to 90% of Japan’s energy has been imported. For&nbsp;
perspective, only about 7% of America’s energy is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:34.680
imported. This has become one of Japan’s biggest&nbsp;
bottlenecks and despite their best efforts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:40.620
they’ve been unable to escape the shackles of&nbsp;
energy dependence. But, how did such an advanced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:45.300
and developed country end up in a situation&nbsp;
where they can’t even keep the lights on?

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:49.140
ENERGY DEPENDENCE:&nbsp;

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:55.080
Taking a look back, Japan’s energy crisis wasn’t&nbsp;
something that developed overnight. It’s something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.080 --> 00:03:00.780
that developed over a century dating back to the&nbsp;
1800s. It all started with a man named Emperor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:07.320
Meiji who rose to power in 1868. When he took&nbsp;
over, much of Japan was filled with a bunch of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:12.780
samurais that were constantly in conflict with&nbsp;
each other. There wasn’t much of a society or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:18.720
an economy, but Emperor Meiji was determined to&nbsp;
change this. He wanted to implement practical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:24.000
imperial rule and focus on economic growth&nbsp;
and enlightenment. One of the first things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:28.740
he did was abolish Japanese domains which&nbsp;
were basically the estates of super powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:34.200
landlords within the empire. Instead, he gave&nbsp;
these powerful landlords positions as governors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:39.960
within the new empire. Emperor Meiji also moved&nbsp;
to eliminate clashes between various armies and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:46.200
clans. He merged all the armes into one Japanese&nbsp;
military and mandated that all Japanese men must&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:51.480
serve atleast four years in the armed forces. He&nbsp;
figured that fighting side by side would help them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:56.940
put aside their differences and work together,&nbsp;
and he was right. The entire area became more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.940 --> 00:04:03.240
united than ever and capitlistic ideologies were&nbsp;
starting to spread. Emperor Meiji even went as far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:08.760
as establishing a national assembly and allowed&nbsp;
Japanese citizens to vote. Clearly, even though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:14.520
Meiji was an emperor by name, he wasn’t what you&nbsp;
would normally associate with emperors. He wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:19.500
a power hungry man that was only concerned about&nbsp;
increasing his own wealth and well being. No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:25.620
he truly wanted to revolutionize Japan and make&nbsp;
the empire a force to be reckoned with. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:31.560
Meiji had all of these crazy ambitions, there was&nbsp;
one thing that he was never willing to do which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:36.300
was give up Japanese independence whether that&nbsp;
be political independence or energy independence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:41.520
This didn’t mean that he blocked out the west&nbsp;
altogether though. He was very much willing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:46.680
to listen to their ideas and take their advice,&nbsp;
but he wanted all growth to originate from Japan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:53.220
itself. And that’s exactly what happened. As the&nbsp;
textile and railroad industries grew, so did the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.220 --> 00:05:00.240
coal production industry. During Meiji’s reign,&nbsp;
Japan’s silk exports grew from 646 tons to 9462&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:07.860
tons. But, their coal production grew even more&nbsp;
from 0.66 million tons to 23.5 million tons. By&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:13.560
the time Emperor Meiji died in 1912, the Japanese&nbsp;
empire had become the most developed nation in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:19.800
east Asia and this kind of got to their head.&nbsp;
They would provoke the United States by bombing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:26.460
Pearl Harbor and that didn’t really end all&nbsp;
that well for them. By the end of WW2, Japan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:32.220
was devastated and occupied by Americans, but&nbsp;
while Japan may have been physically destroyed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:39.000
their spirit and pride was still very much intact.&nbsp;
They may have lost the conquest war, but they sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:44.220
as hell weren’t gonna lose the economic war.&nbsp;
And fortunately for them, the west was very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:48.900
much on their side. Having capitlistic success&nbsp;
stories in the east with Japan and South Korea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:55.260
was very much important for the west and their war&nbsp;
against socialist China and the USSR. So, the west&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.260 --> 00:06:01.860
supported Japan all the way and before you knew&nbsp;
it, the country was booming. There was just one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:08.280
minor issue. Japan’s natural resources couldn’t&nbsp;
keep up with the west supported steroid growth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:13.500
If this was during regular times, Japan probably&nbsp;
would’ve considered more sustainable options,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:19.740
but tensions were high with the Cold War&nbsp;
and Japan had way too much to prove. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:24.840
in 1960, the Japanese government allowed&nbsp;
imported oil to compete with domestic coal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:29.940
as a prime source of energy. And this marked&nbsp;
the beginning of Japan’s energy dependence.

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:35.400
NUCLEAR:
Throughout the 1960s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:41.700
Japan would experience some of most explosive&nbsp;
economic growth numbers ever recorded. Their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:49.020
energy dependence also skyrocketed from 42%&nbsp;
to over 90%, but very few people saw this as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:55.380
a problem. Many people actually saw this as an&nbsp;
advantage. You see, Japanese coal production was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.380 --> 00:07:00.360
extremely expensive relative to the US and&nbsp;
Europe due to poor mining conditions. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:04.440
importing oil from Middle Eastern countries&nbsp;
not only solved Japan’s energy bottleneck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:12.000
but it also reduced energy prices substantially.&nbsp;
At first, this seemed like a win-win scenario up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:19.200
until 1973 when Japan first witnessed the horrors&nbsp;
of energy dependence. This year, OPEC would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:24.120
introduce an oil embargo against the US and all&nbsp;
of their allies which of course included Japan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:29.880
With rising tensions regarding Israel, Japan and&nbsp;
Europe knew that this could be a possibility,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:36.240
and that’s why they stocked up on oil before the&nbsp;
embargo. But, even with that, oil prices in Japan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:42.240
grew four to five fold. For perspective, the&nbsp;
gas price run up that we saw earlier this year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:48.060
was barely a two X from the lows. Fortunately,&nbsp;
this oil embargo only lasted about 5 months,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:53.760
but Japan was very much scarred for life. And&nbsp;
their tone regarding economic growth flipped on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:59.400
its head from maximizing growth no matter what to&nbsp;
just achieving moderate sustainable growth. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:05.700
this would turn out to be nearly impossible. The&nbsp;
energy consumption of Japan had become so large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:10.200
that there was no way for the country to support&nbsp;
themselves based on traditional fuel sources like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:15.900
oil and coal. So, they had think outside the box&nbsp;
and brainstorm newer methods of energy generation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:21.420
and what they landed on was nuclear power. Japan&nbsp;
already had already dabbled with nuclear power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:28.260
in the past having opened their first plant&nbsp;
in 1966. But, after 1973, expanding nuclear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:35.520
power generation became their number one priority.&nbsp;
This was by no means a fast process, but Japan was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:41.460
deteremined. They consistently opened one plant&nbsp;
after another, and within the next 20 years, Japan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:47.460
was able to grow their nuclear power generation&nbsp;
from 3,000 MW to 43,000 MW. This brought down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:55.560
their energy dependent from over 90% down to 78%.&nbsp;
There was obviously still a long way to go, and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.560 --> 00:09:01.500
full on transition would likely take a century,&nbsp;
but Japan was commited and persistent. For them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:07.380
this was no longer a matter of just keeping&nbsp;
energy prices low, it was a matter of maintaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:13.020
economic and political autonomy. You know, it’s&nbsp;
never a good thing when a foreign power can pull&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:18.720
the plug on your entire country. But all of this&nbsp;
progress would be stopped dead in its tracks in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:25.440
2011. This year, Japan would experience the worst&nbsp;
earthquake they ever recorded which caused as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:30.480
tsunami as well. The nuclear power plants would&nbsp;
automatically shutdown as soon as they detected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:35.820
the earthquake, but the subsequent tsunami would&nbsp;
flood the Fukushima plant and lead to radioactive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:41.640
contamination. Over the next few days, 110,000&nbsp;
people would have to be evacuated from nearby&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:47.220
areas and the Fukushima disaster is ranked as&nbsp;
the second worst nuclear disaster in history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:53.220
only beaten out by Chernobyl. I don’t think&nbsp;
you’d be surprised to hear that public sentiment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.880 --> 00:10:00.480
strongly turned against nuclear power following&nbsp;
this incident. With all this opposition, Japan had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:06.000
no choice but to shut down their remaining plants&nbsp;
making them more energy dependent than ever.

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:13.140
A HYDROGEN FUTURE:
With an energy dependence of well over 90%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.140 --> 00:10:19.080
it’s not surprising that Japan has to deal with&nbsp;
conserving power and blackouts despite being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.080 --> 00:10:24.360
a developed country. Japan hasn’t quit trying to&nbsp;
become self sustainable though. They’ve hatched up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:30.600
a new idea involving hydrogen instead of nuclear&nbsp;
power. It turns out that while Japan doesn’t have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:36.720
much coal or oil, they have a bunch of hydrogen.&nbsp;
The ocean floors surrounding Japan are filled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:42.480
with it. This is by no means a new discovery.&nbsp;
The reason that these reserves weren’t already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:47.760
leveraged was due to technological limitations.&nbsp;
You see, the ocean floors aren’t filled with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:53.400
hydrogen itself. They’re actually filled with a&nbsp;
sherbert-like substance that’s often referred to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:59.640
as Fire Ice. To extract this fire ice, you first&nbsp;
have to voyage deep into the ocean where the depth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:05.520
is over a 1000 meters or nearly 3300 feet. From&nbsp;
there, you have to drill hundreds of meters into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:11.040
the sea floor to actually extract the fire ice.&nbsp;
It’s not just plug and play from there either.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:17.400
You then have to extract the methane inside the&nbsp;
fire ice and then convert it to hydrogen. On the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.400 --> 00:11:22.620
brightside, each cubic meter of methane hydrate&nbsp;
is able to produce 160 cubic meters of methane&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.620 --> 00:11:28.380
gas which can be converted to hydrogen. So, you&nbsp;
definitely get a bunch of bang for your buck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.380 --> 00:11:34.080
but that doesn't make it any easier to extract.&nbsp;
The Japanese have been working on a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:38.520
drilling method that can transport the fire ice&nbsp;
through underwater pipelines directly to Japan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:45.300
but that’s still very much in the beta phases of&nbsp;
development. Figuring all of this out is only half&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.300 --> 00:11:50.040
the battle though. The second half of the battle&nbsp;
is actually getting people to use it. It looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.040 --> 00:11:55.740
like they’ve definitely got Japanese companies on&nbsp;
board given that Toyota, Honda, and Nissan are all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.740 --> 00:12:01.680
working on hydrogen vehicles. But, it’s not clear&nbsp;
if Japanese citizens will actually choose these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:07.260
offerings over traditional EVs, especially given&nbsp;
that hydrogen vehicles and the hydrogen itself is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.260 --> 00:12:13.440
rather expensive. Fortunately, Japan isn’t betting&nbsp;
it all on hydrogen. They’re very much working on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:18.600
bringing back nuclear power. Given that it’s&nbsp;
been over a decade since the Fukushima nuclear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.600 --> 00:12:23.820
disaster, tensions have significantly cooled&nbsp;
down, and the public has become much more open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.820 --> 00:12:29.460
to the idea of nuclear power especially when they&nbsp;
see the blackouts first hand. With winter around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.460 --> 00:12:33.660
the corner in the northern hemisphere, people&nbsp;
are likely going to turn up heaters which will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.660 --> 00:12:38.760
only worsen the issue. This is why Japan’s&nbsp;
prime minister is pushing to reopen as many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.760 --> 00:12:43.320
as nine nuclear reactors this winter. But, while&nbsp;
that could help with getting through the winter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.320 --> 00:12:49.560
Japan will still very much be in an energy crunch&nbsp;
for the foreseeable future and that’s why Japan is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:56.100
struggling to keep the lights on. Do you support&nbsp;
nuclear energy? Comment that down below. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

